Dude, I've been playing the game for FOURTEEN YEARS.  And there are
many, many, many, MANY reasons to play nethack other than the
one-shot-dead aspect.  In fact, I'd say that the main reason I care to
investigate the possibility of a "training wheels" experience is
largely because I think that there are a lot of people that would get a
lot out of the game if it wasn't so difficult, especially at the
beginning.  I would imagine that the fact that I have been playing as
long as I have been playing means that I am pretty darn willing to cope
with whatever I get (or don't get) in-game.  The purpose of my posting
was to investigate a., interest, and b., feasibility of creating a
less-difficult experience.  Both appear to be quite high.  Sounds like
you might want to work on that reading comprehension level if you're
ever going to get near ascension.

Not to mention a lack of crying, nor the fact that "writing a script or
something" is a fairly hilarious suggestion.

At any rate, to follow up on my original post: I did some more poking
around in the available guides & tutorials online and it seems that
creating custom levels is not particularly difficult to do.  So I think
I am going to try creating what for the moment I am calling the
Training Wheels Branch, which would basically provide certain extremely
helpful items presented within several levels whose goal is to teach
new players about the game.

Additionally, as I mentioned in my other post here (about custom long
player-triggered speeches), it seems like there are at least two ways
to get instructional text into these levels, if I count creating
engravings on the floors as one of those two, the simpler.  If I can
swing it I will try to dive to the extent of creating quest-esque paged
instructional text as well, but I am not sure if I can do that while
keeping the levels independently bundle-able.  I would prefer to make
it so that users can download just the level files - hopefully, but
again subject to future examiniation, for either vanilla or slash'em -
without having to use a more deeply-customized version of the game
itself.  Obviously the dungeon.def file would require editing which
would require recompiling, but I don't think there's a way for me to
get around that (other than offering binaries with the new levels
precompiled in, which I would almost certainly do.)  Last resort of
course being a simple accompanying text file.

So far for the baby levels idea I've come up with:

- Pet Training Level - where there'd be a store with a bunch of pet
equipment, notably a big stack of tripe :) , a room of "tameables," and
a store with various pet-oriented spellbooks, scrolls, and wands (I
view wands of polymorph as partially pet-oriented)

- Alignment & God-Pleasing Training Level - altar, level-appropriate
sacrificables, [un]holy water and how to create it, donating to priests

- Equipment & Objects Training Level - ways of identification, useful
stuff to look for, enchantments etc.

- Magic-Fighting & Melee-Fighting (either-or or and, I'm not sure yet)
Training Level

The point would be to make the new player still have to work to some
extent to understand what's being presented, but that if the player did
do that work, at least a few extremely helpful objects would be
guaranteed.

Obviously, anyone who DIDN'T want the leg up that such "baby levels"
would offer would be able to ignore them completely.  The way that
bones filenames are generated, in fact, would even allow people to make
sure they did not download bones files that were the result of such
levels.

We'll see how far I get.  I'm pretty certain that I can create the
custom levels with no major difficulty.  The instructional text will be
more of a hurdle.  In the future I'd also like to investigate the
creation of a "baby class" as mentioned above, but that will be
entirely predicated on whether or not I can figure out enough to get it
done and also (more problematically) find the time to do it.

